  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973094/assets/9a7f256ae77447f4b426612a3e136ccb60ebd172a6eb472c9fa9948e324b3085.png)\n\nWhat is the $x$-intercept of the parabola shown above?",
  "worked_solution": "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-8,-3)$ and passes through the point $(-2,-6).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(-8,-3)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(y+3)^{2}=4p(x+8).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-2,-6),$ we substitute $x=-2$ and $y=-6$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-6+3)^{2} & =4p(-2+8) \\\\ 9 & =4p(6) \\\\ p & =\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+3)^{2} & =\\frac{3}{2}(x+8).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0+3)^{2} & =\\frac{3}{2}(x+8) \\\\ 9 & =\\frac{3}{2}(x+8) \\\\ \\frac{18}{3} & =x+8 \\\\ x & =6-8 \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercept is $-2.$",
